Well, my second ascension is done! I'm really happy about this one
because a) it's a Ranger, which I've wanted to ascend for a long time
and b) I managed to keep genoless conduct (and polyselfless, but that
was more by accident.) Oh, and c): you'll notice my character was
named "Firiel," which is rather ironic in this circumstance (the name
is from Tolkien, and it means, essentially, "she who died" or
"mortal." But I like the sound of the name, so I use it anyway.) So
considering that my character made it through without dying even once,
I feel quite happy!

>From what I can remember, the early game was fairly standard. I wrote
Elbereth a lot and used my bow and daggers -- I'd finally gotten a bit
of sense after letting one Ranger after another die in melee combat. I
found a co-aligned altar on an early level, sacrificed a lot and got
Stormbringer, at which point I proceeded to, well, storm through the
dungeon taking out everything in my path. Being chaotic is so much
fun... :-) (I also got a whole host of other artifacts, including
Orcrist, Firebrand, Frostbrand, and something else I can't remember,
but I didn't bother with those much.) I think I got a couple of early
wishes, which got me GDSM and a bag of holding. I did Sokoban in spite
of not planning to -- I've always avoided it in the past, since I
never really wanted to go to the trouble of solving it and felt that
reading a spoiler solution was a copout. But this time I started
pushing boulders around, realized, "Hey, this thing's not too tough
after all!", and almost before I knew it, it was solved. The prize was
a bag of holding which I ended up polypiling, since I had one from my
wish and had found another in the mines. I also got an amulet of
reflection in the big room, which helped a lot.

I went down, established a stash on the level above Medusa, and
cleaned up the dungeon. I wanted to do this ascension in a less messy
fashion than my first one. I also decided to go genoless somewhere
around this point, since I hadn't found any scrolls of genocide and
figured I could probably do it. I hung around the dungeon killing
things until I had enough experience, then went back and did the
Quest. I'd completed it once before, so no surprises there. I got the
Longbow and headed down.

I'd found water walking boots earlier, so Medusa was no problem. I got
to the Castle, killed everything in sight, grabbed the wand (2 charges
IIRC) and got speed boots, oilskin cloak, 2 magic markers, and I can't
remember what else. (I wish Nethack would keep track of what you
wished for as well as how many wishes you use.) I enchanted all my
armor and Stormbringer to +7, plus a stack of daggers to +6. (These
helped a LOT.) I went back up to my stash, polypiled for a while, and
got a saddle, which I immediately put on my red dragon (polymorph trap
a while back). I had this insatiable desire to ride to victory on the
back of a fire-breathing dragon...more on this later...

Gehennom was boring, as usual. The demon lords demanded payment, and I
refused, at which point I proceeded to slice them into very tiny
pieces. :-) One amusing moment: riding on Firebolt (the dragon) I flew
down to Juiblex's level. Quaffed a potion of monster detection...okay,
he's over there. Sat on the upstair and waited for him to attack. He
teleported in, a few squares away. I waited. He didn't move. I waited
some more. Come on, Juiblex, why aren't you attacking? I'm not going
to step within your range so you can get the first hit! Then I
realized...he must not want to engulf me because I'm riding a dragon!
I chortled for a few seconds, then flew over and killed him -- without
getting engulfed!

Then tragedy struck. In the lair of one of the demon lords (Baalzebub
I think) somebody summoned nasty monsters, including two cockatrices.
I wasn't too worried, but then:
The cockatrice bites Firebolt! Firebolt turns to stone!
You are thrown off of Firebolt!
You see here: 
an uncursed saddle
a statue of a red dragon named Firebolt
WHAT?? I couldn't believe my eyes! Wasn't there any way to get my
trusty Firebolt back? I didn't know stone-to-flesh, and wasn't a good
spellcaster anyway. My wand of undead turning was useless here.
Resigned, I decided to tame another dragon as soon as possible.

This became a routine chore. My dragons kept dying! I had to keep
bringing them back to life with my wand. But all the same, I lost one
after the other. Firebolt fell to the cockatrice. Firestorm (another
red dragon) ate a chameleon ("NO, YOU STUPID DRAGON!" I yelled, but
too late) and turned into a gecko. Sigh... Tornado, a black dragon I
tamed in the Wizard's Tower, lasted until the Plane of Earth, when he
died and was no longer tame after I zapped him with the /oUT.
(Actually, I couldn't bear to kill him even then so I just zapped him
with a wand of sleep.) And a jabberwock which I tamed on the Plane of
Air bit the dust fairly quickly -- I don't even remember its name.
After that I gave up trying to tame a flying pet.

Even when I still had a live dragon, it was a huge pain. First of all,
it had a much worse AC than I did and things kept killing it. Every
time it died, I'd have to grab the saddle, zap it with the /oUT, and,
if it came back tame, resaddle it and get back on. If I killed
something and wanted to eat the corpse, I'd have to get off and grab
it as quickly as possible, because the silly beast would eat anything.
And I mean ANYTHING. And the annoying thing is, it would take fifty or
more turns to digest it. So I'd sit at the top of the stairs, waiting
to go down. But no..."Firebolt is still eating." Aarrghh!
Oh, and speaking of dismounting...now THAT was a pain. Because I
hadn't genocided anything, liches came for me in swarms, and my saddle
seemed to be their favorite target. At first I didn't know about the
trick with the wand of opening, and there didn't seem to be ANY way
(short of killing off Firebolt) to get that stupid saddle off. Once I
found out that zapping a wand of opening downwards would do the trick,
this got easier, but it was still a pain, because I'd have to zap the
wand, fall off Firebolt (injuring my legs in the process), grab the
saddle, grab 2 potions of holy water, uncurse and then bless the
saddle (unless I wanted the whole thing to happen again in 2 seconds),
resaddle Firebolt, and get back on. And healing Firebolt was a pain
too: there appears to be no way of throwing a potion *downwards* at a
pet you're riding, so I had to get off Firebolt first before I threw a
potion of extra healing at him. I can't count the number of potions
that just ended up smashing on the floor... And after all that, all my
dragons bit the dust anyway. I have firmly decided NEVER to have a
flying pet again!!

Going genoless didn't make things that much tougher, except for
summoning storms. (Trying to keep my dragons alive was actually more
difficult!) The liches were a pain, especially on the no-teleport
levels, because they could just hit me and teleport away. (NOT FAIR!
If I can't teleport, why are they allowed to?!?) Mind flayers went
down quickly with a few poisoned +5 arrows or +6 daggers. <Grin>

A couple of triumphant moments stand out in my memory. The first was
in Moloch's Sanctum, when a cockatrice was summoned. I killed it and
then proceeded to stone everything on the level, including Rodney, his
double, and the Priest of Moloch. Another was on a no-teleport level,
when I was trying to get rid of an annoying lich which just kept on
appearing, hitting me and then vanishing. It had summoned several
monsters, all of which I killed, except for a black dragon, which I
zapped with a wand of speed monster. Then, staying several paces away
from the dragon, I bided my time. Sure enough, the lich appeared a few
turns later -- directly in line with me and the dragon! "The
disintegration beam misses you. The disintegration beam hits the lich!
The lich is disintegrated!" I shouted in triumph. These stupid things
are resistant to fire, cold, poison, sleep, you name it -- but NOT
disintegration!

The planes went quickly. Rodney showed up once on Earth and then never
again. Before taking the portal to the Astral Plane, I double-checked.
Wearing rings of levitation and free action -- NO conflict. Didn't
want to make the same mistake as last time. A nice pet angel showed
up. Good! I thought. Now I can put on my ring of conflict, right?
After all, it's already here and tame...
Oops. Didn't know that would happen. I thought it was only hostile if
you *showed up* wearing a ring of conflict...I guess not. And
furthermore, it multiplied into three angels! This is not fair! But
they went down fairly quickly due to the hordes of monsters attacking
them (generated by three nasty liches.) After engraving Elbereth, I
sat down and let the monsters kill each other. Famine went down to
four or five +6 daggers. I headed for the left, killing Famine again
along the way. Conflict let me remain relatively unscathed, and when I
entered the temple, things started fleeing. I made my way to the
altar, checked to make sure, and sacrificed the amulet. Done at last!

Various thoughts about this game:
- There really should be a way to smash a potion on a pet's head WHILE
you're riding it. After all, it's right there!
- Saddles should be less tricky to handle. Perhaps it should be
treated like a leash: you're still holding one end of it (the reins.)
That way, if you read a scroll of remove curse, it would be counted as
an item in your inventory.
- I ended up playing a lot of the game as a melee fighter after I got
Stormbringer. I would have used my daggers and the Longbow more, but
they're really not much good in summoning storms. Plus it's so
annoying to pick up the ammunition. And my really big pet peeve is
that you can't really control the number of arrows you shoot/daggers
you throw. Yes, you can tell the computer to throw 2 daggers, for
example, but there should be a way to throw *just as many* daggers as
it takes to kill a creature and no more. So if you can throw maximum
four daggers in a round, and, after throwing the first three, the
monster keels over and dies, you would just refrain from throwing the
last dagger and *not* send it flying onwards to land in the moat and
sink without a trace!
- Also, I wanted to do more spellcasting, but found it impossible due
to the armor situation. It's just too tempting to grab that shield of
reflection.
- Next time I think I'll play a wizard. Time for a spellcasting class.


